DXMT: count the frame latency limit to presentation when vsync or a frame cap holds the game.

This repository's change, written by an AI agent for mavrik (S215). DXMT does not accept code written or
co-written by AI (its CONTRIBUTING.md and AGENTS.md, 2026-07-27), so it is not offered as a pull request; the
finding and the numbers go to DXMT as an issue. Against DXMT 3b252590cb with 0001.

DXMT's frame latency limit (3 frames by default) counts a frame as done when its GPU work finishes. Under vsync or a
frame cap, finished frames then wait in Core Animation's three drawables for their turn on screen, and that wait was
not counted: mavrik's S214 meter measured about 2.4 frames of extra input latency on a light vsync-held game (H3 5.87
frames against G3 3.36, H1 3.89 against G1 1.52). DXVK and Windows count a frame against the limit until it is
presented. mavrik's S215 meter: 2.44 frames less with this change against the same build without it, frame rate held.

- winemetal: MetalDrawable_signalEventWhenPresented (unix call 146, in both call tables) registers a
  presented handler that sets a shared event to the frame's number. The handler runs on a Core
  Animation thread without Wine's thread state, so it only touches Metal, and never lowers the value.
- dxmt: a present carries its frame and whether it waits for presentation (SyncInterval above 0 or
  d3d11.preferredMaxFrameRate set). The encode thread registers the handler before presenting and
  records per frame whether it did. After waiting for frame N - L's GPU work, PresentBoundary also
  waits for its presentation when that frame registered a handler. Vsync off is unchanged: no handler,
  no wait. A wait that passes 100 ms is given up and logged (each time, at most once a second, with a running
  count), and presentation waits stop until a frame is presented again, so a hidden or closing window never
  holds the game.

Not here: releasing the waitable swapchain's object at presentation (it needs a helper thread) and a latency sleep.
.factory/design-passes/2026-10-27-s214/dxmt-latency-plan.md in the mavrik repository has the analysis.

